window.__BAKED={"contractVersion":1,"copy":{"concept":"This site is re-imagined by its visitors","experimentsEmpty":"Coming soon.","tagline":"Creative technology / AI"},"experience":[{"_generated":[],"company":"Anomaly","current":true,"end":"","note":"Sets AI technology strategy and capability across the agency globally, partnering with the Chief AI Officer on vision.","role":"Global Head of AI Technology","slug":"anomaly-global-head-ai","start":"2026-03"},{"_generated":[],"company":"Anomaly","current":false,"end":"2026-03","note":"Built Anomaly's AI practice and the infrastructure under it, taking AI from a novelty to a core part of how the agency works.","role":"Technical Director, Emerging Technologies","slug":"anomaly-technical-director","start":"2023-06"},{"_generated":[],"company":"Active Theory","current":false,"end":"2023-04","note":"Led a team of developers, raised engineering standards, and contributed to the libraries and tooling the studio is known for.","role":"Technical Director","slug":"active-theory-technical-director","start":"2022-04"},{"_generated":[],"company":"Stink Studios","current":false,"end":"2022-04","note":"Shaped the vision for the studio's technology offering: what it sold, and how it positioned technical capability.","role":"Technical Director","slug":"stink-studios-technical-director","start":"2019"},{"_generated":[],"company":"Tool of North America","current":false,"end":"2019-06","note":"Production-side creative director on interactive campaigns for New Balance, The Grammys, Google and Airbnb.","role":"Creative Director of Technology","slug":"tool-creative-director-technology","start":"2014-09"}],"experiments":[],"pages":[{"_generated":[],"body":"I love the creative ways technology shows up in culture, and I've built my career around making them: interactive campaigns for New Balance, The Grammys, Google and Airbnb, a studio's technology offering, and an agency's AI practice. Today I head AI technology globally at the creative agency Anomaly, where I research, teach, prototype and build with AI.\r\n\r\nRight now I'm focused on the first and last mile of enterprise AI, the part that makes or breaks it. Everyone is buying or selling AI on top of company data, and the models are remarkable, but business data grows fast and in every direction until it's too noisy to reason about. The real work  sits outside the model: bringing a company's systems and data into alignment so AI can actually deliver on its promise. ","slug":"","title":"Ben Priddy"}],"projects":[{"_generated":[],"agency":"Anomaly","body":"Rollout is a strategy problem before it is a teaching problem. A week-long format gave each office a shared moment rather than a trickle of lunch-and-learns, and the individual workshops went deeper where people were already curious.\n\nThe aim throughout was adoption, not admiration. A demo that impresses a room once has failed. The test is whether someone uses the thing on Tuesday, unsupervised, on their own work.","client":"Anomaly","contribution":"I co-led the program: AI Weeks for each office, and individual workshops on generative coding, AI video, and automated research.","link":"","media":[],"palette":[],"roles":["Program Lead","Facilitator"],"slug":"ai-education","summary":"A rolling education program across global offices, built to turn current capability into workflows any team could use.","tags":["Education","Applied AI","Internal"],"title":"AI Education","year":"2023-2026","youtube":""},{"_generated":[],"agency":"Anomaly","body":"The fastest way to learn what a new capability is good for is to build with it and watch where it breaks. Over three years this meant a steady run of prototypes and small internal tools, some of which found real use and some of which taught me something and then got switched off.\n\nThat ratio is the point rather than a caveat. Evaluating the tooling landscape from the outside produces opinions; building with it produces knowledge about where the seams are, which is what you need before you put anything in front of a client deadline.","client":"Anomaly","contribution":"I built them, mostly hands-on, and decided which ones were worth hardening and which were worth abandoning.","link":"","media":[],"palette":[],"roles":["Technical Director","Builder"],"slug":"internal-tools-prototypes","summary":"An ongoing run of prototypes and internal tools, built to find out what AI was actually good for inside an agency.","tags":["Tooling","R&D","Internal"],"title":"Internal Tools and Prototypes","year":"2023-2026","youtube":""},{"_generated":[],"agency":"Adam & Eve","body":"","client":"Samsung","contribution":"I acted as creative director, tech director and dev for the project, leading multiple teams and helping coordinate every facet of the project from pitch to community monitoring and adapting, to build to live show run.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":413,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/qledecode/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/qledecode/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/qledecode/loop-1.mp4","width":600}],"palette":["#001d48","#012c6c","#015188","#1a7aba"],"roles":["Creative Director","Tech Director","Support Dev"],"slug":"qledecode","summary":"To celebrate the launch of the much anticipated video game “Cyberpunk 2077”, Adam&Eve and Samsung approached us to create an exciting and authentic experience for superfans to compete for a limited edition Cyberpunk branded Samsung QLED TV. What grew out of this challenge was an exhilarating 2 month long competition that let loyal Cyberpunk / ARG fans across different corners of the internet, decoding messages, avoiding red herrings, even coming to the brink of hacking the contest itself, ultimately landing 5 finalists in a live twitch competition where each competed to remotely hack into a high tech futuristic secure container to win the ultimate prize. The work spanned building various online elements, a pseudo-command line game interface, full experiential and video shoot, fabrication and live show running. Special thanks to all the team, especially VT Pro for handling all of the fabrication and much of the live show run.","tags":["Experiential","ARG","Installation"],"title":"QLEDecode","year":"2020","youtube":"e6PKBbvRYV0"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"","body":"","client":"Spotify","contribution":"I acted as tech director and support dev through all of these efforts, building a solid relationship and work practice with Spotify while leading small dev teams to deliver efficient and high quality work.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":413,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/delightful-moments/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/delightful-moments/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/delightful-moments/loop-1.mp4","width":600},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/delightful-moments/loop-2.jpg","src":"/media/projects/delightful-moments/loop-2.mp4","width":600}],"palette":["#1db954","#23d962","#35e874","#69ff98"],"roles":["Tech Director","Support Dev"],"slug":"delightful-moments","summary":"Over the course of a few years we partnered with Spotify to conceive of and create more than a handful of their marketing microsites, or “delightful moments”. Ranging from playlists for pets to matching the perfect summer song with the perfect summer setting, to just some good old fashioned country music fun, these snackable experiences saw high traffic and high social engagement numbers to continue to strengthen the Spotify brand.","tags":["Web"],"title":"Delightful Moments","year":"2020-2022","youtube":"BHshh9kH36I"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"VML","body":"During the annual week in New York that spotlights fashion trends, New Balance wanted to celebrate individuals who are expressing themselves in independent and distinctive ways. New York Fashion Week provided the background to generate excitement for the Fresh Foam Cruz Nubuck shoe. We created real-time exception spotting technology that scanned crowds of people, gathered inputs, and identified the anomalies and exceptions to the norm. In the week leading up to the activation on September 6, computer vision technology captured statistics about fashion choices of thousands of pedestrians in New York's Soho neighborhood. On the day of the event, this data was used to identify who the exceptions to the norm were.For the activation, we set up a camera on a corner in Soho that constantly streamed its video into an AI system that analyzed the clothing.The computer vision technology followed a series of steps: 1. It detected human figures, and cut each detected person into a separate image. 2. Used facial detection to determine whether the person was facing the camera or not. We didn't want people missing the experience as they passed by! 3. The technology then found the person's hips, shoulders, and knees to cut out images of body regions to identify different articles of clothing. For example, the shoulders to the hips would be a person's top. 4. The images were sent through a a set of style detection models to determine color, pattern, shape, and more.5. These results were then compared with the data gathered the week before the event to determine how uniquely dressed the person was.The computer vision technology recognized details like patterns, styles, and accessories, as well as colors and shapes. Unlike editorial street photography, the technology removed bias from finding the individuals who stood out from the crowd. During the real-time activation, the pedestrians identified as unique were broadcast on a large screen and given a pair of New Balance sneakers.","client":"New Balance","contribution":"I acted as tech director, creative director and support dev for the project, guiding the client, agency and internal team through important considerations when implementing a computer vision system in the wild. The tech team consisted of myself and one other dev who specializes in openFrameworks. The biggest problems I guided the team in solving were how to efficiently and reliably represent fashion from video data captured in the wild, how to efficiently and reliably detect attire on pedestrians in real time and how to plan the environment and construct the system to perform fast enough to keep up with the brisk walking pedestrians of NYC.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":413,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":150,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-1.mp4","width":750},{"alt":"","height":160,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-2.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-2.mp4","width":638},{"alt":"","height":150,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-3.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-3.mp4","width":172},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-4.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-4.mp4","width":1000},{"alt":"","height":150,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-5.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-5.mp4","width":750},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-6.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-6.mp4","width":794},{"alt":"","height":150,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-7.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-7.mp4","width":800},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-8.jpg","src":"/media/projects/exception-spotting/loop-8.mp4","width":1010}],"palette":["#e458a5","#e882bf","#f59acf","#f2bfdd"],"roles":["Creative Director","Tech Director","Support Dev"],"slug":"exception-spotting","summary":"Fashion is such an electric and ephemeral thing that it’s almost undefinable. We cast leaders of fashion as oracles capable of divining what is exceptional, but even they are limited by their own biases. How can we truly know what exceptional fashion is? New Balance wanted to experiment with this idea at New York Fashion week 2018. So we created an AI-driven activation where pedestrians were analyzed in real-time and compared against local fashion data to try to find the truly unique. When a real exception stepped off the side walk as a normal person they landed onto their own personal 10 second catwalk as a star. The storefront lit up with their moving image broadcast for all to see, celebrating them as scientifically exceptional and truly fashionable.","tags":["AI","Experiential","Computer Vision"],"title":"Exception Spotting","year":"2018","youtube":"NBurrBaouxQ"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"TBWA/CHIAT/DAY","body":"","client":"The Grammys","contribution":"I acted as tech director, creative director and support dev for the project, guiding the client, agency and internal team through important considerations in implementing computer vision driven AR in a moving vehicle. The tech team consisted of myself and one other dev who specializes in openFrameworks. After helping the client avoid suboptimal environments for installing public AR we decided on AR-in-a-car. From this point I led the team in prototyping and running many tests to establish guidelines on car speed, route, display technology, etc. Additionally I worked with the client and composer to plan a dynamic software musical instrument to generate structured music while ingesting computer vision data and outputting visual AR in real-time.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":410,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/play-the-city/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":150,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/play-the-city/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/play-the-city/loop-1.mp4","width":800}],"palette":["#364356","#5a5a5a","#666f7b","#9ba3ad"],"roles":["Tech Director","Creative Director","Support Dev"],"slug":"play-the-city","summary":"New York City is a place so alive with music that rhythm and melody resonate from literally everything and everyone there. The Recording Academy wanted to capture this musicality to celebrate the return of the Grammys to New York in 2018. So we created an AR experience that used an Uber as the playhead moving through the natural score unfolding in the city outside the backseat window. Every person, car, light and many other objects appeared in their rhythm and position to compose the real-time music and AR visuals of the streets and sidewalks of New York.","tags":["AR","Experiential","Computer Vision"],"title":"Play the City","year":"2018","youtube":"rfAdsEE3Wj8"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"","body":"","client":"Nike","contribution":"I acted as tech director on this project, planning architecture, real time data, leading a full stack dev team on the site build, coordinating client and third party integrations, and supporting the full 24 hour event (whew).","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":413,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/air-max-day/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/air-max-day/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/air-max-day/loop-1.mp4","width":600}],"palette":["#aba7a9","#d4d2d3","#eeeeee","#ffffff"],"roles":["Tech Director","Support Dev"],"slug":"air-max-day","summary":"How does Nike celebrate its annual splashy event Air Max Day in the height of a global pandemic? By creating its first global online event ever. We partnered with Nike to create a 1 day online event bringing together interesting content from all around the globe, and allowing those who got access to become virtual world travelers, moving from city to city catching content releases, live streams and stamping their digital passport along the way. Hundreds of thousands of global attendees traveled billions of miles to be a part of this one of a kind experience.","tags":["Web"],"title":"Air Max Day","year":"2021","youtube":"QUI2mJgV-GQ"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"","body":"","client":"NYTimes","contribution":"I acted as tech director, leading the dev team, consulting and supporting design and ux teams, and partnering with the client to ensure the design, cms and site met their needs.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":413,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/ad-nytimes-com/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/ad-nytimes-com/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/ad-nytimes-com/loop-1.mp4","width":600},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/ad-nytimes-com/loop-2.jpg","src":"/media/projects/ad-nytimes-com/loop-2.mp4","width":600}],"palette":["#e3c50b","#ffe334","#fce75d","#fff3a6"],"roles":["Tech Director","Support Dev"],"slug":"ad-nytimes-com","summary":"The New York Times approached us with the task of upgrading, updating and consolidating a suite of websites within their advertising services ecosystem. We worked together to design and implement a component based, fully cms controlled site.","tags":["Web"],"title":"ad.nytimes.com","year":"2022","youtube":"w08rZwtJ-b8"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"Universal McCann","body":"","client":"U.S Army","contribution":"I acted as creative director and game developer on this project guiding a team of 6 developers working together to create 7 WebGL mini-games combining to form a challenging online mission. During the concepting phase I co-led the planning of the entire property, from the story and how it fits in the US ARMY universe, to conceiving of and designing games that would test relevant knowledge and skill in a concise and engaging way, to sourcing and interviewing expert consultants to vet and influence specific subject matter and game mechanics for better realism. As game developer I built 2 of the 7 games. One game tested the recruit’s spatial and structural competence as a demolitions expert in a mountainous WebGL terrain, overlaid by an invisible hand created physics environment for the explosions and projectiles to interact with. The other game challenged the recruit to problem solve through the 4th wall while trying to discover and input passcodes a “safe-guard bug” would infect the system and the only way for the recruit to combat it was to open the javascript console, track down the traveling bug in standing memory and kill it before it literally attacked and killed the code of the site.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":891,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/cryptaris/hero.jpg","width":1887},{"alt":"","height":148,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/cryptaris/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/cryptaris/loop-1.mp4","width":800},{"alt":"","height":150,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/cryptaris/loop-2.jpg","src":"/media/projects/cryptaris/loop-2.mp4","width":150},{"alt":"","height":148,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/cryptaris/loop-3.jpg","src":"/media/projects/cryptaris/loop-3.mp4","width":800}],"palette":["#000000","#2b2b2b","#353535","#777777"],"roles":["Creative Director","Tech Lead","Game Developer"],"slug":"cryptaris","summary":"Motivated young people, with all the information in the world at their fingertips, have already seen and created so much before even leaving high school these days. They are hyper-capable but also hyper-finicky with their time, so if you try to intrigue them with a challenge, make sure it’s very intriguing, and very challenging. The US ARMY wanted to micro-target this talent pool and promote their STEM based careers. So we created Cryptaris, a web-based, STEM focused simulated mission where users must use fast thinking, creative problem solving and high level skills to succeed. With help from experts in relevant fields we conceived of challenges ranging from kinematics, to medicine, to programming and more. The result was an award winning game so challenging that less than .1% were able to finish.","tags":["Web"],"title":"Cryptaris","year":"2015","youtube":"ViihEBZppUk"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"","body":"","client":"Stink Studios","contribution":"I acted as tech director, driving architecture decisions, guiding the design team and consulting with stakeholders to achieve the best possible result.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":413,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/stinkstudios-com/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":200,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/stinkstudios-com/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/stinkstudios-com/loop-1.mp4","width":600}],"palette":["#000000","#555555","#555555","#969696"],"roles":["Tech Director","Support Dev"],"slug":"stinkstudios-com","summary":"To support a major rebrand, we (Stink Studios) rebuilt our site from the ground up. Prioritizing simple motion and stark branding, we created a simple component based and cms driven site to highlight the work.","tags":["Web"],"title":"stinkstudios.com","year":"2022","youtube":"CGzg7ge21Xc"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"Commonwealth","body":"","client":"Chevrolet","contribution":"I acted as tech director, creative director and core developer for the project, guiding the client on decisions on how to stably integrated computer vision into the brochure experience. As core developer I built the entire front end in WebGL to be beautiful and performant on both desktop and mobile.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":413,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/meet-bolt-ev/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":148,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/meet-bolt-ev/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/meet-bolt-ev/loop-1.mp4","width":542},{"alt":"","height":400,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/meet-bolt-ev/loop-2.jpg","src":"/media/projects/meet-bolt-ev/loop-2.mp4","width":1280}],"palette":["#964686","#b57293","#c786a7","#e6bbd1"],"roles":["Creative Director","Tech Director","Lead Developer"],"slug":"meet-bolt-ev","summary":"The traditional car brochure in the dealership is classic but has become stale. Commonwealth McCann and Tool partnered together to give it a tech facelift. By using computer vision we were able to create a mobile experience that is activated by a simple scan of the brochure at a local Chevy dealership. The scan leads to supporting video content to better explain car features and ultimately to product sales at the point of purchase. As the second half of the experience the user was able to explore the look of the vehicle in a robust WebGL care configurator.","tags":["Web","Computer Vision"],"title":"Meet Bolt EV","year":"2017","youtube":"TXFymuB9HPI"},{"_generated":[],"agency":"","body":"","client":"Airbnb","contribution":"I acted as creative director and developer on this project, collaborating with the highest levels at airbnb to conceive of how to make an interactive globe fun and discoverable. Myself and one specialist WebGL dev built a site that progressively managed memory on globe covered in sprite animations and high res zoom tiles.","link":"","media":[{"alt":"","height":403,"kind":"image","poster":"","src":"/media/projects/belong-anywhere/hero.jpg","width":735},{"alt":"","height":150,"kind":"loop","poster":"/media/projects/belong-anywhere/loop-1.jpg","src":"/media/projects/belong-anywhere/loop-1.mp4","width":804}],"palette":["#00a29f","#19d7d8","#57dadc","#77e7e8"],"roles":["Creative Director","Developer"],"slug":"belong-anywhere","summary":"Airbnb truly has global presence. Their homes and experiences pop up in every part of the world, just waiting for people to discover. To mark New Year’s Eve 2014, Airbnb wanted to share this idea with the public in a fun, light hearted and explorable way. So we created The Belong Anywhere Globe: an explorable, zoomable WebGL earth packed with custom illustrations of people, bears, camels, pyramids, skyscrapers and more. As you pan around to see pulsing dots correlated to real geolocated reservation data you really get the feel of just how global Airbnb is.","tags":["Web"],"title":"belong anywhere","year":"2015","youtube":"LzkFcIcrOxo"}]};
